  "id": "Compound::DB04573 - Gene::5243",
  "verbalization": "Estriol, through its role in causing Endometrial Hyperplasia and Pelvic Venous Thrombosis, is linked to Abcb1 via a series of molecular interactions. The evidence suggests that Estriol may influence the expression or activity of Abcb1 through the activation of steroid receptors, as it is involved in the regulation of steroid metabolism. Both Endometrial Hyperplasia and Pelvic Venous Thrombosis are associated with the binding of steroids to Abcb1, indicating a potential mechanism by which Estriol indirectly affects Abcb1 function. The shared lowest common ancestors—Organic Cyclic Compound, 3-Hydroxy Steroid, and Steroid—suggest a common pathway in steroid metabolism, which may underlie the observed interactions. The evidence supports a hypothesis that Estriol, through its role in steroid-related processes, may modulate Abcb1 activity, though the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated.",
